{
 "_about": "A few real words for each kanji, chosen to show the same character taking different readings. Seeing 日本 (に) beside 三日 (か) beside 日 (ひ) is what makes the on/kun split concrete -- far more than being told that it exists.",
 "_why": "The reading cards deliberately teach one standalone reading, which is right for a beginner but leaves them unable to explain why 日 is ひ alone and something else in every compound. These examples fill that gap without adding another thing to memorise: they are shown, never tested.",
 "_format": "vocab[kanji] = [{w: word, r: reading in kana, m: English, kind: how THIS kanji is read in this word}]. kind is on, kun, or irregular -- the last for words like 今日 きょう and 大人 おとな, where no per-character reading applies. kr is the slice of that word's reading belonging to THIS kanji (日 in 三日 is か), which is what the compound-reading drill asks about; it is null where no clean slice exists.",
 "_scope": "Curated for the beginner range rather than generated from a dictionary: a handful of well-chosen words teaches the on/kun contrast, where dozens of entries would bury it. Add your own in userdata/vocab.local.json, same shape.",
